WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:07.080
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.060
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.900
About 6 months ago, I got a comment asking about
how falling works in general relativity.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.940
Bob, I know, 6 months is a long time,

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.780
but this whole series of quantum videos happened

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.640
and, when the crazy train calls,
you’ve just got to ride it.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.580
Wooo! Wooo!

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.740
Stop making excuses, Nick.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.250
Anyway, I’m ready now.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.620
Let’s kick this pig!

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.300
We’re going to start with the classical
version of gravity

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.040
or the “Old-School” version as we say
on the hip side of physics.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.100
No one says that.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.180
I say that!

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.740
I want to make sure everyone is on the same
page.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.040
Example Time!

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.100
Say a squirrel falls from a tree.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.740
There’s a force of gravity pulling it down
and a bit of air drag resisting the fall,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.340
but we’ll be ignoring that air drag for
simplicity.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.200
Physicsland! Ah!

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.960
As the squirrel falls, the gravity stays very
steady.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.000
Eventually, the squirrel impacts the ground,
which exerts an upward force on the squirrel,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.440
resisting gravity.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.060
The squirrel slows down and stops.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:08.360
This all makes perfect sense.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.540
It’s just an interplay of forces.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.260
But, in a previous video, we took a look at
the history of relativity.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.040
We did a couple thought experiments

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.720
Gedanken Versuch!

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.920
that made Einstein’s relativity completely
unavoidable.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.780
The ultimate conclusion was:
Gravity isn’t a real force.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.600
And, Prat, that doesn’t mean gravity doesn’t
exist.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.840
It’s just not a force.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.100
There’s a difference.

00:01:30.109 --> 00:01:33.789
The truth is gravity is just curvature in
space-time,

00:01:33.789 --> 00:01:36.600
a process governed by Einstein’s Equation.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.540
That equation looks like this,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.940
but, put more simply, like this.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.920
A physicist once explained this equation by
saying:

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.560
“Energy tells space-time how to curve.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.200
Space-time tells energy how to move.”

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.080
which I can’t say any better myself.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.540
That equation looks pretty interesting, but
it... it might leave you asking:

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.060
What the [BEEP] does that mean?

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.920
Well, that’s what this video is for.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.320
I should start by leveling with you though.

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:04.799
Einstein’s equation outlines the fundamental
principle of curved space-time

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.880
and it’s used in a few math steps when working
with general relativity.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.520
But it’s not actually very useful beyond
that.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.320
We’re going to get a much better understanding
of how things like squirrels

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.260
move in curved space-time by talking about geodesics.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.940
You mean that guy that’s dating Sofia Richie?

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:22.940
What?

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.520
No, that’s Scott Disick.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.340
I said geodesic!

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.580
Let’s… let’s just get back to the squirrel.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.680
In old-school physics, gravity is a force
pulling down objects

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.720
until another force stops them.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.680
But if we trust general relativity, gravity
isn’t a force.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.830
So why on Earth would the squirrel even bother
falling?!

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:47.140
The answer: It’s following a geodesic,
a straight line in curved space-time.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.920
But there’s a lot of stuff in that statement
we need to unpack.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.520
First, anything that’s under the influence
of only gravity

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.740
is following one of these geodesic paths.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.180
That makes sense for the squirrel because
its path looks straight.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.920
But a satellite orbiting the Earth is also
traveling along a geodesic.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.540
No one is really saying that an orbit is straight.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.350
We’re just saying that now we talk about 
geodesic paths,

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:13.840
where we used to talk about straight ones.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.640
It’s a replacement concept.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.500
A geodesic is the new straight line.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.540
Second, it’s not curved space.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.240
It’s curved space-time!

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.160
And that time part is what matters most for the
falling squirrel.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.500
This is why it’s important to avoid visuals
like ribbons wrapped around shapes

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.860
and definitions about minimizing or maximizing
distance or time.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.560
They’re not necessarily wrong, but they’re
probably going to mislead you.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.140
So then what’s a Geodesic?

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.300
Let’s start with Newton’s laws and go
from there.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:47.300
Newton’s second law says acceleration is
caused by an unbalanced force

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:51.400
or net force equals mass times acceleration.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.860
In old-school physics, the squirrel accelerates
toward the ground

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:58.730
because gravity is an unbalanced force pulling
it down.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:03.100
When the ground finally pushes up,
the gravity is balanced and the acceleration

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.480
drops to zero.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.000
But in general relativity, there is no gravity
force,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.660
so Newton’s second law no longer makes sense.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:14.840
That’s where curvature comes in
and, by far, the biggest curvature for the

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.340
squirrel is time curvature.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.980
The squirrel doesn’t fall because there’s
a force.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:24.560
It falls simply because its future is on the
ground.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.220
That’s what a Geodesic is.

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:30.300
It’s just a path you take when there aren’t
any unbalanced forces on you,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.560
which is exactly what a straight line used
to be.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.400
In fact, that equation is called the Geodesic Equation.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.980
It’s usually written as a differential equation,
but, conceptually, those are the same thing.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:46.520
At the Earth’s surface, that curvature actually
equals 9.8 meters per second squared,

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.229
the free fall acceleration we’ve observed
for centuries.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:55.080
The squirrel will travel along that geodesic
until a force tells it not to

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.920
like one from the ground.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:02.480
None of you are on a geodesic path right now
because you all have a force underneath

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:03.540
holding you up.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.640
Unless, of course, you’re watching from the International Space Station.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:07.880
In which case, wow!

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.430
Thanks for watching the show!

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.650
Anyway, things don’t really fall because
of a force of gravity.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:17.700
They fall because time curvature puts their
future at different place in space.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.840
From our point of view, that just happens
to look like a force.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.620
Because we can’t see space-time as it really
is.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.460
So, got any questions about general relativity?

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.820
Please ask in the comments.

